# Project Instructions

Example for a TypeScript web app using the Codex adapter. Adapt paths and
commands to the target project; do not copy this blindly.

## Build

```bash
npm run typecheck
npm test -- --runInBand
npm run lint
npm run build
```

## Architecture

- Keep UI components in `src/components/` and route handlers in `src/routes/`.
- Put shared data access in `src/lib/`; route code should not call external APIs
  directly when a library wrapper exists.
- Prefer existing test utilities in `test/` before adding new fixtures.

## Harness

### Agent Routing

Users do not need to name harness skills. Route ordinary requests by intent:

- "Apply meta-harness to this project" or "set up agent memory/traces" means initialize or update this project harness.
- "This keeps failing", "stop repeating this", or "make this not happen again" means inspect raw traces and evolve the harness before another retry.
- "Review this carefully", "am I missing anything?", or other high-stakes judgment requests mean use multi-perspective review when it materially reduces risk.
- "Try variants", "optimize this", or "keep the measurable winner" means propose an autoresearch loop only when a fixed evaluator and clear metric exist.

For ordinary feature work, work normally. Escalate only when the request or
evidence matches one of the routing signals above.

### Trace Root

Use `.harness/traces/` for harness history:

```text
.harness/traces/evolution/     # Harness change history
.harness/traces/failures/      # Failure diagnosis with raw context
.harness/traces/experiments/   # Autoresearch episodes
.harness/traces/search-set.md  # Active verification cases
```

The global Codex trace root at `${CODEX_HOME:-~/.codex}/harness/traces` is
cross-project agent memory only. It can record routing/install mistakes that
span projects, but it does not replace this project's active trace root or
search-set guards.

If this project was migrated from Claude Code and `.claude/traces/` contains
meaningful history, keep using it until an explicit migration trace moves the
history into `.harness/traces/`.

### Search-Set Policy

Before and after harness changes, run Active verify commands from
`.harness/traces/search-set.md` when practical. A good initial Active case for
this app is:

```markdown
### SS-001: TypeScript contract regression
- **Source**: initial Codex harness setup
- **Symptom**: Agent changes compile locally but break project type contracts.
- **verify**: `npm run typecheck`
```

### Change Strategy

Use additive changes first, subtractive changes second, structural changes last.
Keep one functional harness change per iteration so regressions are attributable.

### Multi-Review Governance

Use multi-review as an advisory global skill for high-stakes judgment. Claim
governance-grade multi-review PASS only if this project declares meta-harness
governance and has `scripts/check-multi-review-result.py`. If governance is
declared but the validator is missing, report setup incomplete. If governance is
not declared, keep the result advisory/non-governance.

### Autoresearch

Use the `autoresearch` skill only for measurable hypothesis -> experiment ->
evaluate loops with a fixed evaluator, protected evaluator files, an experiment
log, and episode traces. Do not use autoresearch for ordinary feature work or
subjective UI review without a fixed evaluator.

## Codex Notes

- Sandbox mode: record the actual Codex sandbox mode for the project.
- Writable roots: record any path outside the repo that Codex may write.
- Network: default to no network for verification unless a command explicitly
  requires it.
- Escalation policy: request approval before running commands that need broader
  filesystem access, network, credentials, or unsafe side effects.
- Skipped verification: record SKIPPED with the blocked reason and rerun command;
  never treat skipped verification as PASS.
